# 0G-Delphi — deployment and operations environment
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored and is read automatically by every script in `scripts/`.
# Keeping keys here rather than on a command line is the point: an
# `export DEPLOYER_KEY=0x...` typed at a prompt lands in shell history, and in an
# agent-driven session it lands in a transcript too.
#
# ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ WHAT TO FILL IN, IN ORDER                                                 │
# │                                                                           │
# │  §1  five keys/addresses — required for ANY deployment beyond anvil       │
# │  §2  mainnet-only, and the deploy REFUSES to proceed without them         │
# │  §3  knobs with sane defaults; leave them alone unless you need them      │
# │  §4  the web UI, which lives in frontend/.env.local — copied, not shared  │
# └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
#
# ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ ONLY ONE OF THESE IS A PRIVATE KEY.                                       │
# │                                                                           │
# │   DEPLOYER_KEY     PRIVATE KEY   it signs the deployment                  │
# │   GOVERNANCE       ADDRESS       signs later, from its own wallet         │
# │   GUARDIAN         ADDRESS       signs later, from its own wallet         │
# │   TREASURY         ADDRESS       never signs anything — it just receives  │
# │   CURATOR_SIGNER   ADDRESS       the contract checks a signature against  │
# │                                  it; the key stays wherever it lives      │
# │                                                                           │
# │ An address is 0x + 40 hex characters. A private key is 0x + 64.           │
# │                                                                           │
# │ The four addresses are PUBLIC. Their private keys must never be in this   │
# │ file, and after the handover the deployer's key has no power either — the │
# │ timelock owns everything, and governance drives the timelock.             │
# │                                                                           │
# │ Paste a key where an address belongs and the deploy stops and says so, by │
# │ name. It cannot un-write the file, so rotate that key if it happens.      │
# └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
#
# Every address below is left as a bare `0x` on purpose. That parses as the zero
# address, and the deploy refuses it by name — so a role you forgot fails loudly
# instead of quietly shipping to nobody.


# ═══ §1 · ROLES ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Five separate keys. On a testnet you MAY point them all at one wallet — the
# deploy allows it, because a testnet lifecycle often wants one key driving
# everything. On MAINNET the deploy refuses any role the deployer holds, and
# refuses a guardian that is also governance. See contracts/test/unit/DeployRoles.t.sol.
#
# Test the separation on Galileo FIRST, with five genuinely different wallets.
# A rehearsal where every role is the same key rehearses nothing.

# ── 1.1 Deployer ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sends the deployment and pays gas. Needs ~0.15 0G on Galileo; the faucet at
# https://faucet.0g.ai gives 0.1 0G per wallet per day, so fund it a day ahead.
# The `0x` prefix is optional — the scripts add it if missing.
#
# After the handover it owns NOTHING. Its only lasting power is whatever you
# also list it as below, which on mainnet must be nothing.
# ▸ PRIVATE KEY (0x + 64 hex)
DEPLOYER_KEY=

# ── 1.2 Governance ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Becomes the timelock's proposer and executor; the timelock becomes the owner of
# ConfigRegistry, MarketFactory, AgentRegistry and ResolutionModule. Intended to
# be a MULTISIG — nothing in the deploy can check that it is one, which is why
# the distinctness rules exist.
#
# Every parameter change and every upgrade goes through it, after a 48-hour delay.
# ▸ ADDRESS (0x + 40 hex) — not a key
GOVERNANCE=0x

# ── 1.3 Guardian ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A single key BY DESIGN (spec §13.3). It may `pause()` and `void()` a market
# that has not closed, and nothing else — it cannot move funds and cannot change
# an outcome. Fast action should not need several signatures; changing the rules
# should. MUST differ from GOVERNANCE on mainnet.
# ▸ ADDRESS (0x + 40 hex) — not a key
GUARDIAN=0x

# ── 1.4 Treasury ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Receives the protocol's fee share, slashed resolver stake, forfeited dispute
# bonds, and anything swept from a market nobody claimed. A plain address; it
# never needs to sign anything.
# ▸ ADDRESS (0x + 40 hex) — not a key
TREASURY=0x

# ── 1.5 Curator ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The only key that can approve a market for creation, by EIP-712 signature.
# CURATOR_SIGNER is the ADDRESS the contract checks; CURATOR_KEY is the private
# key behind it, needed only by `scripts/e2e-market.sh`, which has to produce a
# real signature.
#
# If the curator is the deployer, leave CURATOR_KEY empty and it falls back.
# On mainnet the curator must NOT be the deployer, so this key must be filled in
# — or the lifecycle script simply is not run against mainnet, which is fine.
# ▸ ADDRESS (0x + 40 hex) — not a key
CURATOR_SIGNER=0x
# ▸ PRIVATE KEY, and the ONLY other one. Needed solely by the lifecycle
#   script, which has to produce a real EIP-712 signature. Leave it out
#   entirely if you are not running that script against this chain.
# CURATOR_KEY=


# ═══ §2 · MAINNET ONLY ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── 2.1 Collateral ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The token markets settle in. Left empty on a testnet, the deploy creates a
# MockUSDC with an OPEN `mintTo` — anyone can print it, which is exactly why it
# is refused on mainnet. There, this must name a real token with code at it, and
# the deploy stops if it does not.
# COLLATERAL=0x

# ── 2.2 Direct-settlement bypass ────────────────────────────────────────────
# A key allowed to settle a market WITHOUT a committee. Empty by default, and
# refused outright on mainnet. Useful only for a testnet lifecycle where staking
# a committee is not the thing under test; a market settled this way records
# `viaCommittee == false`, so an operator's decision can never be mistaken for a
# committee's.
#
# LEAVE THIS EMPTY for the Galileo rehearsal if you want to exercise the real
# committee path (scripts/setup-committee.sh + packages/agent-kit resolve).
# RESOLVER=0x


# ═══ §3 · KNOBS ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── 3.1 Networks ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ZERO_G_TESTNET_RPC=https://evmrpc-testnet.0g.ai
# ZERO_G_MAINNET_RPC=https://evmrpc.0g.ai
#
# Chain ids, for reference: Galileo 16602 · mainnet 16661 · anvil 31337.
# `EXPECTED_CHAIN_ID` makes scripts/deploy-galileo.sh refuse a chain it was not
# pointed at. Set it when you have several RPCs configured.
# EXPECTED_CHAIN_ID=16602

# ── 3.2 Governance delay ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 48 hours, which is what spec §13.3 asks for. Lower it on a testnet only if you
# intend to rehearse an upgrade end to end and cannot wait two days.
# TIMELOCK_DELAY=172800

# ── 3.3 0G Storage ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where MarketSpec documents and settlement receipts live. A market's `specRoot`
# is a document's Merkle root, so without an upload the root addresses nothing
# and the market has no readable question.
# ZG_INDEXER=https://indexer-storage-testnet-turbo.0g.ai
#
# Force an upload on a chain that is not Galileo. Off by default, because
# uploading pays gas on 0G Chain and a local anvil run has nothing to pay with.
# ZG_UPLOAD=1

# ── 3.4 0G Compute ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The inference provider an agent asks. Run `node scripts/setup-compute.mjs`
# with no --provider to print the live catalogue; do NOT hardcode one in an
# agent, because the catalogue shifts and a dead address fails only at request
# time.
#
# Setting this up costs real 0G, and the numbers are contract minimums rather
# than suggestions: 3 0G to open the ledger, 1 0G moved into the provider's
# sub-account. Both are recoverable through `retrieveFund` after the inference
# contract's 24-hour lock. The inference itself costs millionths of a 0G.
# ZG_PROVIDER=0xa48f01287233509FD694a22Bf840225062E67836

# ── 3.5 The lifecycle script ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Seconds of trading before a market can be closed. On a public chain the script
# genuinely waits this long, so keep it small when you are watching.
# TRADING_WINDOW_SECONDS=180
#
# 0 = FAST (committee of 1), 1 = VERIFIED (5, threshold 3),
# 2 = DETERMINISTIC (3, threshold 2). The tier decides the committee's SHAPE, so
# a run with three staked resolvers wants tier 2 — tier 1 reverts
# NotEnoughResolvers.
# TIER=1
#
# Create a market and stop, leaving it Open for an agent to trade against.
# Without this the script closes and settles what it created, which leaves
# nothing to buy.
# STOP_AFTER_CREATE=1
#
# Skip the confirmation prompt in scripts/deploy-galileo.sh.
# ASSUME_YES=1


# ═══ §4 · THE WEB UI ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# These are NEXT_PUBLIC_*, read by the browser bundle, so they live in
# `frontend/.env.local` rather than here — and that file is gitignored by
# `frontend/.gitignore` (`.env*`), which is why this block is a comment and not a
# template. Copy it across, then restart the dev server; Next reads env at boot.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_DATA_MODE=indexer
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL=https://evmrpc-testnet.0g.ai
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_CHAIN_ID=16602
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_MARKET_FACTORY=<MarketFactory from deployments/16602.json>
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_FROM_BLOCK=<deploymentBlock from that same file>
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ZG_INDEXER=https://indexer-storage-testnet-turbo.0g.ai
#
# `mock` needs none of them and is the default. `chain` reads state only;
# `indexer` adds history rebuilt from event logs. NEXT_PUBLIC_ZG_INDEXER is
# optional and separate from the RPC — without it questions and settlement rules
# read as unavailable, which is true rather than merely missing.
#
# Browse http://localhost:3003, NOT 127.0.0.1: Next 16 blocks cross-origin
# dev-resource requests and the page hangs on its skeleton with a clean console.
